+#pragma OPENCL EXTENSION cl_khr_fp16 : enable
+
+// Direct 1D convolution for the Kokoro vocoder, written for Adreno.
+//
+// ggml's kernel_conv_2d stages both operands through `local` memory with two
+// barriers per K-block. That is the right shape for a desktop GPU and the
+// wrong one here: Adreno has no fast local scratchpad, and that kernel
+// measures ~19 GFLOPS on a device that does 473. This one holds the output
+// tile in registers, issues no barrier, and lets the L1 cache do the sharing.
+//
+// Register tiling alone still left it issue-bound rather than bandwidth-bound
+// (1.6 GB/s of an available 24), because of how many load instructions a tap
+// costs. Two changes fix that, worth 1.8x together:
+//
+//   * The weights are repacked to [Cin, KW, Cout]. In ggml's own layout the
+//     TS_K output channels one work item needs sit 2*KW*Cin bytes apart -- one
+//     load and one cache line each. Repacked they are contiguous and 32-byte
+//     aligned, so they arrive in TS_K/8 vload8s.
+//   * The input window is rotated in registers instead of re-read: consecutive
+//     taps overlap in VEC-1 samples, so the fast path costs one load and VEC-1
+//     register moves per tap rather than VEC loads.
+//
+// Twenty load instructions per 64 MADs becomes three. Do not be tempted to
+// vload the input as well: xrow + lo + kw is only 4-byte aligned, and this
+// driver scalarises a misaligned vector load into something far slower than
+// the loads it replaces (measured on the weights: 443 ms -> 1482 ms).
+//
+// Layout, first axis contiguous in each:
+//   src [W, 1, Cin] f32, knl repacked [Cin, KW, Coutp] f16, dst [OW, 1, Cout] f32.
+// The repacked weights are padded from Cout to Coutp, a multiple of TS_K, so a
+// work item always has a whole aligned tile to load; only the store is masked.
+// The fast path needs s0 == 1 and d0 == 1; strided and dilated convolutions
+// take the general path in the same kernel, still with repacked weights.
